1. e4 e5 2. Nc3 { This is the starting point of this chapter. With the 2.Nc3 White can opt for three different setups: 1) Some kind of delayed Bishop's Opening with Bc4 2) The Glek Opening with g3 3) Vienna Gambit with f4. } Nc6 { 2...Nf6 is much more popular but we want to have ...f5 move against the Glek Opening. } 3. g3 { The Glek Opening, which is not a particularly threatening variation. White's setup generally includes Bg2, d3, Nge2, etc. Black has various acceptable ways of developing his forces, but the one including quick f5 is especially promising, as shown by the following lines: } Bc5 4. Bg2 d6 { Getting ready for ...f5. } 5. Nge2 { -KEY- } f5 $1 6. d3 Nf6 7. O-O O-O 8. Na4 { Going after the bishop has been almost the only move played by White. It's difficult to suggest anything else really. } fxe4 9. Nxc5 dxc5 10. dxe4 b6 { With an edge. White's pieces are passively placed, whereas Black's are ready for active play, e.g. ...Ba6 (b7), ...Qe8, ...Rd8, ...Nd4 (b4). The bishop pair offers White no real benefits here, as his bishops are too restricted. } 
