1. e4 e5 2. Nc3 { This is the starting point of this chapter. With the 2.Nc3 White can opt for three different setups: 1) Some kind of delayed Bishop's Opening with Bc4 2) The Glek Opening with g3 3) Vienna Gambit with f4. } Nc6 { 2...Nf6 is much more popular but we want to have ...f5 move against the Glek Opening. } 3. f4 { This looks like a version of the King's Gambit but it's actually called the Vienna Gambit. It's probably objectively a dodgy line and Black can come out on top. -KEY- } exf4 4. Nf3 g5 { We just play like in the King's Gambit. } 5. g3 Bg7 6. d4 d6 7. gxf4 $2 { This is a blunder. } g4 ( { and it's difficult to move the knight as } 7... g4 7. Ng5 h6 { and } 8. Ng1 Qh4 ) 

