1. e4 e5 2. Nc3 { This is the starting point of this chapter. With the 2.Nc3 White can opt for three different setups: 1) Some kind of delayed Bishop's Opening with Bc4 2) The Glek Opening with g3 3) Vienna Gambit with f4. } Nc6 { 2...Nf6 is much more popular but we want to have ...f5 move against the Glek Opening. } 3. f4 { This looks like a version of the King's Gambit but it's actually called the Vienna Gambit. It's probably objectively a dodgy line and Black can come out on top. } exf4 4. Nf3 g5 { We just play like in the King's Gambit. } 5. g3 Bg7 6. d4 d6 7. d5 { -KEY- } Ne5 8. gxf4 { Now White regains the pawn but in the process ends up in a weakened position. } gxf4 9. Bxf4 Bg4 10. Bb5+ Kf8 { It's not an ideal place for our king but there are other factors which determine the evaluation. } 11. Be2 Nxf3+ 12. Bxf3 Qf6 13. O-O Qxf4 14. Bxg4 Qg5 $15 { Strong dark-square control gives Black the better game. } 
